BATCH={'key':'ia_aadv','label':'Iowa: a first scatter','added':'2026-11-19 22:22'}
PLACES=[
# ---- Northeast: the Driftless hills ----
N('motor-mill-iowa','Motor Mill','Elkader','ia','industry','standing',42.80703,-91.35139,'site','open','walk','','1867–1891',
  'A six-storey limestone flour mill, about 90 feet tall, built from 1867 for the new town of Motor on the Turkey River, with stone quarried from the bluff above and lowered in cable cars on wooden rails. It was finished in 1869 and ground flour, cornmeal and feed, but chinch bugs, competition, a washed-out narrow-gauge railroad and the 1883 flood that took the dam ruined it; the court auctioned it in 1891. The Klink family farmed the townsite from 1903 and sold the machinery; Clayton County Conservation bought it in 1983.',
  'The mill and four stone outbuildings of the late 1860s (the inn, the stable, the cooperage and an icehouse among the buildings the county names). Wikipedia repeats the story that three walls are of rounded stone and the fourth of square-cut stone, each by a different mason; the north wall is flat because a woolen mill was meant to stand against it. The mill\'s interior is shown only on guided tours; the machinery was sold off after 1903 (interior otherwise not checked). Trails, primitive camping and river access round it.',
  'A ninety-foot stone mill built for a town that never came.',
  'Clayton County Conservation Board park (Motor Mill Historic Site), southeast of Elkader on the Turkey River; the Motor Mill Trail runs from Turkey River Park in Elkader along the Highway 13 shoulder to Grandview Road. The grounds are open to the public. The county\'s page gives guided tours Saturday and Sunday noon to 5 p.m., Memorial Day to Labor Day, free with donations welcome, and the visitor centre on the inn\'s ground floor Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. in the same season; motormill.org says tours run on summer Saturdays and several fall weekends. Pit toilets; bow hunting only, in the south unit. A small lot by the mill road (OpenStreetMap\'s mapped lot point fell in trees on the aerial and was not used).',
  [W('Motor Mill Historic District'),L('Clayton County Conservation: Motor Mill Historic Site','https://claytoncountyconservation.org/motor-mill-park'),L('Motor Mill Historic Site: about','https://motormill.org/about')],
  brick='no',
  hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day: guided tours Saturday and Sunday noon to 5 p.m.; visitor centre Monday to Friday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m.'),
N('fort-atkinson-iowa','Fort Atkinson','Fort Atkinson','ia','fort','standing',43.14556,-91.93917,'site','public','walk','forts','1840–1849',
  'A U.S. Army post begun on May 31, 1840 on the Neutral Ground, set up to keep the Ho-Chunk (Winnebago), moved here from Wisconsin, on that land and to keep traders and settlers off it. Finished in 1842 with 24 buildings inside an 11-foot-9-inch stockade and 14 more outside, it was held by Iowa volunteers after the regulars left for the war with Mexico in 1846, and abandoned in February 1849 when the Ho-Chunk were removed again. The state bought it in 1921, began rebuilding in 1958 and made it a state preserve in 1968.',
  'A stockade of wood and stone round the footprint of the fort, and the buildings that survive: the North Barracks, the northeast and southwest cannon houses and the powder magazine, in limestone quarried nearby. A small museum (guns, tools, clothing, a canoe, letters and orders) opens by appointment only. The Fort Atkinson Rendezvous is held the last full weekend of September.',
  'The Army post built to hold a removed nation on a strip of land, and given up when they were removed again.',
  'Iowa DNR state preserve at 303 2nd Street, Fort Atkinson, managed from Volga River State Recreation Area. The DNR page gives the fort open Memorial Day weekend through the Rendezvous at the end of September, no admission charge, and daily hours of 4:30 a.m. to 10:30 p.m. year-round; the museum by appointment (563-425-4161). Parking on 2nd Street beside the fort.',
  [W('Fort Atkinson State Preserve'),L('Iowa DNR: Fort Atkinson State Preserve','https://www.iowadnr.gov/places-go/state-parks/all-parks/fort-atkinson-state-preserve')],
  brick='no',
  hours='Grounds daily 4:30 a.m. to 10:30 p.m.; fort open Memorial Day weekend to the end of September; museum by appointment'),
N('montauk-clermont','Montauk (Governor Larrabee\'s house)','Clermont','ia','landmark','standing',43.00833,-91.64871,'exact','open','drive','','1874–1965',
  'A two-storey, 14-room Italianate house built in 1874 on a hill over the Turkey River for William Larrabee, banker, state senator for 18 years and twice governor of Iowa, who fought the railroads over their rates. His wife wanted a house on the hill for fear of malaria by the river. Their daughter Anna lived here until her death in 1965, and the family gave the house, its contents and 40 acres to the state in 1976.',
  'The house as the family left it, with its original furnishings, shown by the State Historical Society; bricks kilned in Clermont and stone from the Williams quarry nearby. Four bronze statues of generals, Ulysses S. Grant among them, stand in the yard. Trails on the grounds look over the Turkey River valley.',
  'A governor\'s house left exactly as his family lived in it for ninety years.',
  'State Historical Society of Iowa historic site (Montauk State Preserve), 26223 Harding Road, northeast of Clermont off US 18. Free admission. Hours from the society\'s page: daily noon to 4 p.m. Memorial Day weekend to Labor Day, then Friday to Sunday noon to 4 p.m. to October 31. The same page carries a general notice of museum and research-centre hours that seems to be for the society\'s Des Moines building; the site hours above are the ones listed under Visiting Information.',
  [W('Montauk (Clermont, Iowa)'),L('State Historical Society of Iowa: Montauk Historic Site','https://history.iowa.gov/history/sites/montauk-historic-site')],
  brick='?',hours='Memorial Day to Labor Day daily noon to 4 p.m.; Labor Day to October 31 Friday to Sunday noon to 4 p.m.'),
N('shot-tower-dubuque','Dubuque Shot Tower','Dubuque','ia','industry','standing',42.50008,-90.654,'site','public','walk','','1856–1911',
  'A 120-foot tower built in 1856 to make lead shot: molten lead poured through a grate at the top fell into water at the bottom as round pellets. The Panic of 1857 ruined it almost at once; a St. Louis company bought it and left it idle, and after the Civil War the Standard Lumber Company used it as a fire lookout. Fires in 1911 that ended the local lumber trade burned out its wooden interior and top. Dubuque citizens restored it in 1961 and it was repaired again from 2004 with the riverfront.',
  'The tower itself, one of the last shot towers in the country, beside the Mississippi Riverwalk; the DAR marker of 1963 is at its foot and an information sign on site. The interior woodwork was lost in 1911 (whether the inside can be visited was not checked). The old Star Brewery building stands next to it. The 2025 photograph in the Wikipedia article shows a stone lower shaft and a red brick upper part.',
  'Where Dubuque dropped molten lead 120 feet to make bullets, for three years.',
  'On the riverfront at East Commercial Street, Port of Dubuque, in view from the public Mississippi Riverwalk (who owns the tower itself was not checked); the HMdb marker page puts the marker on East Commercial Street about 0.3 miles east of East 7th Street. Hours not checked (open ground).',
  [W('Shot Tower (Dubuque, Iowa)'),L('HMdb: Dubuque Shot Tower marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=43129')],
  th=[42.49861,-90.65444],thn='Lot off Veterans Drive, Port of Dubuque',brick='some'),
N('wapsipinicon-mill','Wapsipinicon Mill','Independence','ia','industry','standing',42.46889,-91.89528,'site','open','drive','','1867–1976',
  'One of the largest grist mills in Iowa, built from 1867 by millwright Samuel Sherwood for the Independence Mills Company, which a woman, Clara B. Snow, and 32 businessmen of the town formed in March 1867. Its foundation is of glacial granite boulders pulled from the prairie and cut on site, over basement walls of limestone; part of it is the foundation of the first mill here, of 1854. It ground for more than a century, changing over from millstones and water wheels to rollers and steam, and stopped in 1976, when the Buchanan County Historical Society took it on as a museum.',
  'Six storeys and 102 feet from foundation to roof: a heavy timber frame inside a brick wall eight inches thick, on the granite-and-limestone foundation, at the west end of the Main Street bridge. Inside, hand-hewn timbers with mortise-and-tenon joints, square nails and wooden pegs; the horse-drawn stone puller used to haul the boulders stands outdoors. Only the main floor is accessible, with a film of all six floors.',
  'A brick mill on a foundation of prairie boulders, grinding for 109 years.',
  'Owned and run by the Buchanan County Historical Society as a museum, at the west end of the Main Street bridge over the Wapsipinicon River in Independence. Hours from the society\'s brochure page; telephone 319-334-4616. Street parking and a lot on 1st Street West.',
  [W('Wapsipinicon Mill'),L('Buchanan County Historical Society: Wapsipinicon Mill brochure','https://www.buchanancountyhistory.com/?p=114')],
  brick='yes',hours='Mid-May to mid-September, Tuesday to Sunday noon to 4 p.m.'),
# ---- East: the Mississippi and Iowa City ----
N('pine-creek-gristmill','Pine Creek Gristmill, Wildcat Den State Park','Muscatine','ia','industry','standing',41.4675,-90.8675,'site','open','walk','','1848–',
  'Benjamin Nye, one of the first settlers here after 1833, built mills on Pine Creek from the 1830s and put up the present mill in 1848 for $10,000, with timber from his own sawmill. He was murdered by a son-in-law in 1852. A water turbine drove it until 1878, with a steam engine after that when the water ran low. The Pine Mill Bridge was put up beside it in 1876, and Wikipedia calls this the only one of Iowa\'s many bridge-and-mill pairs still in place.',
  'A three-storey mill of native oak beams joined with pegged mortise and tenon, with a two-storey addition. Inside are three milling plants: a three-stand double roller mill for wheat flour on all three floors, a triple roller mill for corn, and a pair of 36-inch grindstones for buckwheat. The 1876 Pine Mill Bridge and the one-room Melpine schoolhouse are in the park too. The DNR calls it the oldest working grist mill between the Mississippi and the Rockies.',
  'A mill whose builder was murdered by his son-in-law, still grinding in a state park.',
  'Iowa DNR, Wildcat Den State Park, 1884 Wildcat Den Road, east of Muscatine; park office 563-263-4337. The DNR page gives the mill open for tours Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. and Sunday noon to 5 p.m., May through early October; the Friends of Pine Creek Grist Mill run a gift shop in it. Lot on Wildcat Den Road by the mill.',
  [W('Pine Creek Gristmill'),W('Wildcat Den State Park'),L('Iowa DNR: Wildcat Den State Park','https://www.iowadnr.gov/wildcatden')],
  th=[41.46645,-90.86854],thn='Mill lot, Wildcat Den Road',brick='no',
  hours='May to early October: Wednesday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday noon to 5 p.m.'),
N('old-capitol-iowa-city','Old Capitol','Iowa City','ia','civic','standing',41.66114,-91.53605,'exact','open','walk','','1840–',
  'Iowa\'s territorial and first state capitol: the cornerstone was laid on July 4, 1840, the last four territorial legislatures met here, and it was the seat of state government from statehood in December 1846 until the capital moved. John F. Rague, who had designed the 1837 Illinois capitol, is credited with the design. The University of Iowa restored the interior to its 1850s state over six years from 1970, as a museum. In November 2001 a contractor\'s torch set the cupola on fire and the dome was destroyed; it reopened in May 2006.',
  'The gold-domed building of pale stone (as its Wikipedia photograph shows) at the centre of the Pentacrest. Inside, rooms restored to the 1850s and a ground-floor museum on the building, the university and the state. Admission free; opening times not found on the museum\'s pages (see access).',
  'Iowa\'s first capitol, whose dome burned in 2001 under a contractor\'s torch.',
  'The University of Iowa\'s Old Capitol Museum, on the Pentacrest in downtown Iowa City. Always free. The museum\'s visit pages read in this session gave events but no opening times: hours not checked. City and university ramps nearby (the Dubuque Street ramp is about 450 m southeast).',
  [W('Iowa Old Capitol Building'),L('Old Capitol Museum: visit','https://oldcap.uiowa.edu/plan-your-visit')],
  brick='no',hours='?'),
# ---- Southeast: the Villages of Van Buren ----
N('van-buren-county-courthouse-iowa','Van Buren County Courthouse','Keosauqua','ia','civic','standing',40.73333,-91.96353,'exact','open','drive','','1840–',
  'Begun in July 1840 after Keosauqua won the county seat, and finished in September 1843 by Edwin Manning (who later built the Hotel Manning) for $6,712. It is the oldest courthouse in Iowa and the only one the county has used; the Villages of Van Buren call it the second oldest in the United States in continuous use. The first death sentence in Iowa was passed here in 1845. Its tower and two circular walnut staircases were taken out in the 1800s; the interior was restored in 1981 to 1983.',
  'A brick building on an oak frame, with walls 22 inches thick below and 18 above. The second-floor courtroom, still in use, was long the largest in Iowa without columns; walnut trim, a single staircase that replaced the two spiral ones, portraits of judges and old maps. The county office building to the south dates from 1896.',
  'Iowa\'s oldest courthouse, where the state\'s first death sentence was passed.',
  'Van Buren County, 902 4th Street, Keosauqua. The Villages of Van Buren page says it is open to the public in business hours and the courtroom can be toured when court is not in session (319-293-3129); days and times not given. Small public lots by Main Street, about 350 m south.',
  [W('Van Buren County Courthouse (Iowa)'),L('Villages of Van Buren: Van Buren County Courthouse','https://villagesofvanburen.com/explore/van_buren_county_courthouse/')],
  brick='yes',hours='County business hours (times not given); courtroom tours when court is not sitting'),
N('bentonsport-iowa','Bentonsport','Bentonsport','ia','town','standing',40.72505,-91.85378,'area','public','walk','','1836–',
  'A steamboat port on the Des Moines River, platted in 1836 and named for Senator Thomas Hart Benton, at the first lock and dam the territory authorized in 1839. It had grist mills, a saw mill, Iowa\'s first paper mill (1852) and about 1,000 people, then faded after the railroad reached Des Moines in 1866; the river was declared non-navigable in 1870 and the dam failed in 1879. Fires and floods took the mills. About 40 people live in it now, and 16 blocks of the old village became a historic district in 1972.',
  'Buildings of the 1840s to 1860s on the old blocks: the Mason House Inn (late 1840s, a steamboat hotel, now a bed and breakfast with tours by appointment), the limestone Stone House of 1851 to 1854, the Federal-style Greef house of 1863 with folding interior shutters in butternut, and the Presbyterian church. The 1883 truss bridge to Vernon, the oldest wagon bridge of its type left on the Des Moines River, is open as a footbridge, and a rose garden fills the old lock. The village brochure warns that many buildings are private homes not open for tours.',
  'A steamboat town left almost as it was when the boats stopped.',
  'Unincorporated village on county road J40 between Keosauqua and Bonaparte; public streets, a river park and the footbridge. Shops and the Mason House keep their own hours (not checked). Villages of Van Buren publish a self-guided walking-tour brochure. Pin is the village centre near the bridge.',
  [W('Bentonsport'),L('Villages of Van Buren: Bentonsport National Historic District','https://villagesofvanburen.com/villages/bentonsport_national_historic_district/'),L('Self-guided tour of historic Bentonsport (PDF)','https://villagesofvanburen.com/files/bentonsport_walking_tour_50916.pdf')],
  brick='?'),
# ---- Central and south ----
N('roseman-covered-bridge','Roseman Covered Bridge','Winterset','ia','landmark','standing',41.29279,-94.14975,'exact','public','drive','','1883–',
  'One of the last two covered bridges Madison County built, by Harvey P. Jones and George K. Foster in 1883 over the Middle River, for $2,930. It carried traffic for almost a hundred years until a new bridge bypassed it in 1981, and was renovated in 1992. Local legend made it a "haunted bridge" after an 1892 posse chase; the film The Bridges of Madison County made it famous in 1994.',
  'A Town lattice truss with a queenpost frame laid over it, of native lumber with plank floor, on iron or steel cylinder piers, under the flat roof Jones gave his bridges; the siding is boards painted red with white trim. The Iowa DOT gives it as 225 feet long, Madison County Tourism as 104 feet. It stands in place as the centrepiece of a small park.',
  'The bridge from the film, and Iowa\'s haunted one.',
  'Madison County\'s bridge (the Iowa DOT page gives the county as its jurisdiction); on Elderberry Avenue (address 2451 Elderberry Ave.) over the Middle River about 7.5 miles southwest of Winterset. Closed to traffic since 1981; a small park round it. Parking not checked on the aerial yet.',
  [W('Roseman Covered Bridge'),L('Madison County Tourism: Roseman Covered Bridge','https://www.madisoncounty.com/portfolio/roseman-covered-bridge-1883'),L('Iowa DOT: Roseman Bridge','https://iowadot.gov/historicbridges/historic-bridges/roseman-bridge')],
  brick='no'),
N('terrace-hill-des-moines','Terrace Hill','Des Moines','ia','landmark','standing',41.58321,-93.64912,'exact','open','walk','','1866–',
  'A Second Empire mansion with a 90-foot tower, built from 1866 to 1869 for Benjamin Franklin Allen, Iowa\'s first millionaire, to designs by the Chicago architect William Boyington; it had running hot and cold water, indoor toilets, an elevator and gas light. F.M. Hubbell bought it in 1884 and his family offered it to the state in the 1970s; Governor Robert Ray moved in in 1976, and it is still the governor\'s residence. A National Historic Landmark since 2003.',
  'The house and its carriage house on a hill over downtown. Hour-long guided tours begin with a film in the Carriage House (exhibits on the Hubbells), then go through the residence, with a stained glass window on the stair landing, and the gardens in good weather; the third floor and the governor\'s office are not shown.',
  'Iowa\'s first millionaire\'s house, now the governor\'s.',
  'State of Iowa, 2300 Grand Avenue. Guided tours only, by reservation at least 48 hours ahead (515-281-7205); $5 adults, $2 ages 6 to 12, cash or check. Closed state holidays, and closed for tours November 17 to 27, 2026. Enter by the pedestrian gate near the flag pavilion on Forest Drive, where a trooper admits guests. Lot at Forest Drive and Grand Avenue, and free street parking.',
  [W('Terrace Hill'),L('Terrace Hill: touring information','https://terracehill.iowa.gov/visit')],
  brick='?',hours='March to December: Tuesday to Saturday tours at 10:30 a.m. and noon (also 1:30 p.m. in December), by reservation 48 hours ahead'),
N('buxton-iowa','Buxton (marker)','Lovilia','ia','town','marker',41.14053,-92.90793,'site','public','drive','','1895–1927',
  'A coal town the Chicago and North Western Railway\'s Consolidation Coal Company built to fuel its trains, and in its day the largest unincorporated town in the country and the largest coal town west of the Mississippi. Most of its people were Black, many recruited from the South; in 1905 it had 2,700 Black and 1,991 white residents, with European immigrants among them, and Black doctors, lawyers and teachers. Fires in 1916 and the closing of the mines emptied it; the post office closed in 1923 and the last people had left by 1927. Wikipedia dates the town to 1895, the state marker to 1900.',
  'A State Historical Society marker of 1978 in the small triangle park in Lovilia. The townsite itself, listed on the National Register in 1983, lies in the country east of Lovilia; Wikipedia says most of it is now forest or farmland with a few ruins. No source read in this session says the townsite is open to visitors.',
  'An integrated coal town of 6,000 that disappeared within thirty years.',
  'The marker is in the triangle park between Iowa Route 5 and North East Avenue in Lovilia, about 0.1 mile north of East 22nd Street (HMdb). The townsite is not public as far as any source read says: treat it as private and look only from the road.',
  [W('Buxton Historic Townsite'),L('HMdb: Buxton marker','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=176639')],
  brick='?'),
# ---- West: the Missouri River ----
N('squirrel-cage-jail','Squirrel Cage Jail (Pottawattamie County Jail)','Council Bluffs','ia','civic','standing',41.25779,-95.85198,'exact','open','walk','','1885–1969',
  'A rotary jail built in 1885 for about $30,000 to a patent of 1881 by William H. Brown and Benjamin F. Haugh of Indianapolis: pie-shaped cells on three floors turn inside a cage, so one jailer with a hand crank could bring each cell to the single door without walking among the prisoners. It was one of 18 such jails and the only three-storey one, and held prisoners until 1969. The park board bought it in 1971; the county historical society has owned it since 1977. A National Historic Landmark since 2023.',
  'The 90,000-pound revolving cell block, kept much as it was when the county closed it in 1969, with prisoners\' names and dates scratched into the cell walls; the front part of the building held the jailer\'s offices, kitchen, trusty cells and women\'s quarters. One of three revolving jails left in the country.',
  'A three-storey jail that turned on a crank.',
  'Historical Society of Pottawattamie County museum, 226 Pearl Street, Council Bluffs. No reservations needed. Admission $10 adults, $8 seniors and students, $5 ages 6 to 18, $6 public safety workers; members free. Street parking downtown (not checked).',
  [W('Pottawattamie County Jail'),L('Historical Society of Pottawattamie County: Squirrel Cage Jail','https://www.thehistoricalsociety.org/museums/squirrel-cage-jail.html')],
  brick='yes',hours='Thursday to Saturday 10 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday 1 to 4 p.m.'),
N('woodbury-county-courthouse','Woodbury County Courthouse','Sioux City','ia','civic','standing',42.49708,-96.406,'exact','open','walk','','1916–',
  'Built from July 1916 to March 1918 for $850,000 to designs by George Grant Elmslie with William L. Steele and William Gray Purcell, after the county outgrew its 1870s Second Empire courthouse. Wikipedia quotes it as "one of the finest Prairie School buildings in the United States"; it is a National Historic Landmark (1996) and still the county\'s courthouse.',
  'A nearly square four-storey block of Roman brick with granite and terra cotta trim and a 157-foot, eight-storey tower; Alfonso Iannelli\'s sculpture over the doors and Elmslie\'s metal grillwork at the north entrance. The county welcomes visits to the public areas with a camera, and arranges guided tours by request; it has video tours of the dome room, the 8th floor and the jail. The brick is a light brown in the photograph on Wikipedia, not red. Interior otherwise not checked.',
  'A Prairie School tower built as a county courthouse.',
  'Woodbury County, 620 Douglas Street at Seventh Street, Sioux City. The county\'s tours page says visitors may come in to see and photograph the public areas, and guided tours are arranged through the Board of Supervisors (712-279-6525, ext. 2); opening times not given. A lot on 8th Street, about 140 m north.',
  [W('Woodbury County Courthouse'),L('Woodbury County: courthouse tours','https://www.woodburycountyiowa.gov/100/tours/')],
  th=[42.49827,-96.40539],thn='Lot on 8th Street',brick='no',hours='?'),
# ---- North ----
N('park-inn-hotel-mason-city','Historic Park Inn Hotel and City National Bank','Mason City','ia','hotel','standing',43.15153,-93.20167,'exact','open','walk','','1910–',
  'Two joined buildings designed by Frank Lloyd Wright for the law partners James E. Blythe and J. E. E. Markley, his first work in Iowa, finished in 1910: a 42-room hotel and a bank with law offices between them. Wikipedia calls it the last of the six hotels Wright designed still standing, and the bank one of two Wright banks left. It was on Iowa\'s most-endangered list in 1999; the nonprofit Wright on the Park, formed in 2005, bought and restored it, including the brick and terra cotta front and the art glass skylights.',
  'The Prairie School hotel and bank on Central Park, a working hotel again. Wright on the Park gives tours of the hotel and bank (and of the Rock Crest-Rock Glen houses nearby) by booking, and keeps a gift shop in the hotel. Interior beyond that not checked.',
  'The last Frank Lloyd Wright hotel left standing.',
  'Owned by Wright on the Park, 15 West State Street, Mason City (641-423-0689). The hotel takes guests; tours are booked through wrightonthepark.org. The hours below are for its gift shop and office. Public lots on North Washington Avenue and behind the block.',
  [W('Park Inn Hotel'),L('Wright on the Park','https://wrightonthepark.org/'),L('Wright on the Park: about and hours','https://wrightonthepark.org/about')],
  brick='?',hours='Gift shop and office: Tuesday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 3 p.m., Sunday and Monday 9 a.m. to 1 p.m.; tours by booking'),
]
